1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:02,880
Walk through Manhattan on a cold morning,

2
00:00:02,920 --> 00:00:04,590
and you'll see something strange.

3
00:00:04,860 --> 00:00:06,690
Steam, pouring out of the street.

4
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:10,920
Rising from manholes, and from tall orange
and white chimneys standing in the middle

5
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:11,630
of the road.

6
00:00:12,572 --> 00:00:14,612
Most people think it's
coming from the subway,

7
00:00:14,652 --> 00:00:15,982
or the sewers.

8
00:00:16,192 --> 00:00:16,742
It isn't.

9
00:00:16,852 --> 00:00:19,652
It's coming from a hidden system,
more than a hundred years old,

10
00:00:19,692 --> 00:00:21,802
that most New Yorkers never think about.

11
00:00:22,670 --> 00:00:25,460
Under the streets of New York
there's a whole second city.

12
00:00:25,690 --> 00:00:26,900
Pipes full of steam.

13
00:00:27,490 --> 00:00:30,520
Tunnels carrying water from a
hundred and twenty-five miles away.

14
00:00:30,730 --> 00:00:33,600
A subway that never stops,
and pumps that never rest.

15
00:00:34,535 --> 00:00:36,135
So what's really under New York City?

16
00:00:36,175 --> 00:00:38,345
And what would happen
if it stopped working?

17
00:00:41,265 --> 00:00:42,275
Let's start with the steam.

18
00:00:42,743 --> 00:00:46,953
In eighteen eighty-two, a company
started burning coal in Lower Manhattan,

19
00:00:47,143 --> 00:00:51,223
boiling water, and sending the steam through
pipes under the streets to heat nearby

20
00:00:51,263 --> 00:00:51,853
buildings.

21
00:00:52,323 --> 00:00:54,413
It began with just a few miles of pipe.

22
00:00:55,303 --> 00:00:58,833
Today, that system is run by
the energy company Con Edison,

23
00:00:59,063 --> 00:01:01,933
and it's the largest steam
system in the United States.

24
00:01:02,403 --> 00:01:05,393
More than a hundred miles of
steam pipes lie under Manhattan.

25
00:01:06,356 --> 00:01:09,146
It heats and cools around
seventeen hundred buildings:

26
00:01:09,396 --> 00:01:12,856
offices, hotels, hospitals,
museums and apartment blocks,

27
00:01:12,896 --> 00:01:16,706
all the way from the southern tip of
Manhattan up to Ninety-Sixth Street.

28
00:01:17,646 --> 00:01:21,276
The steam leaves the plants at more
than four hundred degrees Fahrenheit.

29
00:01:21,786 --> 00:01:24,176
Buildings use it for
heating, for hot water,

30
00:01:24,266 --> 00:01:26,656
and even to run their air
conditioning in summer.

31
00:01:27,618 --> 00:01:29,028
So why does it come out of the street?

32
00:01:29,278 --> 00:01:30,548
Sometimes a pipe leaks.

33
00:01:31,018 --> 00:01:35,468
More often, rainwater or groundwater touches
a hot steam pipe and instantly boils.

34
00:01:35,778 --> 00:01:39,398
The orange and white stacks are put over
those spots to carry the steam up above the

35
00:01:39,438 --> 00:01:41,368
traffic, so drivers can still see.

36
00:01:42,223 --> 00:01:43,953
Most of the time, it's harmless.

37
00:01:44,423 --> 00:01:46,713
But on the eighteenth of
July, two thousand seven,

38
00:01:46,963 --> 00:01:49,963
a steam pipe under Lexington
Avenue, near Grand Central,

39
00:01:50,003 --> 00:01:50,713
exploded.

40
00:01:51,684 --> 00:01:55,604
A geyser of steam and mud shot
higher than the nearby skyscrapers,

41
00:01:55,644 --> 00:01:57,434
and a crater opened in the street.

42
00:01:57,824 --> 00:01:59,434
A tow truck fell into the hole.

43
00:01:59,664 --> 00:02:01,834
One person died, and dozens were injured.

44
00:02:02,184 --> 00:02:04,254
The pipe had been laid
in nineteen twenty-four.

45
00:02:06,874 --> 00:02:11,464
But to understand why New York sends its
water through tunnels a hundred miles long,

46
00:02:11,574 --> 00:02:13,664
you have to go back to
a time when it didn't.

47
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:17,820
Two hundred years ago, New Yorkers drank
from wells and from a pond in Lower

48
00:02:17,860 --> 00:02:19,770
Manhattan that had become filthy.

49
00:02:20,240 --> 00:02:23,940
In eighteen thirty-two, cholera swept through
the city and killed around thirty-five

50
00:02:23,980 --> 00:02:25,070
hundred people.

51
00:02:25,380 --> 00:02:26,610
Dirty water helped it spread.

52
00:02:27,476 --> 00:02:29,926
So the city did something
enormous for its time.

53
00:02:30,176 --> 00:02:34,196
It built an aqueduct about forty miles
long to bring clean water from the Croton

54
00:02:34,236 --> 00:02:34,686
River.

55
00:02:35,076 --> 00:02:37,376
In eighteen forty-two, the water arrived,

56
00:02:37,416 --> 00:02:41,546
flowing almost entirely by gravity,
and the city threw a huge celebration.

57
00:02:42,055 --> 00:02:45,585
New York uses about a billion
gallons of water every day.

58
00:02:45,715 --> 00:02:48,325
And almost none of it comes
from anywhere near the city.

59
00:02:49,099 --> 00:02:53,149
It comes from nineteen reservoirs and
three lakes in the mountains to the north,

60
00:02:53,319 --> 00:02:56,369
some of them more than a hundred
and twenty-five miles away.

61
00:02:57,215 --> 00:02:58,605
And here's the clever part.

62
00:02:58,795 --> 00:03:01,005
Those reservoirs sit higher than the city.

63
00:03:01,415 --> 00:03:05,695
So about ninety-five percent of New York's
water travels all the way to your tap by

64
00:03:05,735 --> 00:03:08,495
gravity alone, through
aqueducts and giant tunnels,

65
00:03:08,535 --> 00:03:09,705
without a single pump.

66
00:03:10,628 --> 00:03:14,988
The water is so clean at the source that
New York is one of only a handful of big

67
00:03:15,028 --> 00:03:17,558
American cities allowed
to skip filtration.

68
00:03:17,968 --> 00:03:19,918
It's disinfected, but not filtered.

69
00:03:20,810 --> 00:03:24,930
Inside the city, the water spreads through
about sixty-eight hundred miles of water

70
00:03:24,970 --> 00:03:25,540
mains.

71
00:03:25,610 --> 00:03:29,620
And the used water leaves through about
seventy-five hundred miles of sewers.

72
00:03:29,790 --> 00:03:33,780
Put together, that's enough pipe to cross
the United States more than four times.

73
00:03:34,210 --> 00:03:36,720
But gravity can only push water so high.

74
00:03:36,850 --> 00:03:39,780
Once it reaches the city, it
can climb about six stories.

75
00:03:40,050 --> 00:03:41,600
Above that, buildings need help.

76
00:03:42,368 --> 00:03:44,598
That's why the skyline is full of these.

77
00:03:44,768 --> 00:03:45,878
Wooden water towers.

78
00:03:46,168 --> 00:03:50,568
The city requires buildings taller than
six stories to pump water up into a tank on

79
00:03:50,608 --> 00:03:54,038
the roof, and then gravity sends
it back down into every apartment.

80
00:03:55,048 --> 00:03:57,188
There are somewhere around
ten thousand of them,

81
00:03:57,228 --> 00:04:00,518
and many are still made of wood,
the way they were a century ago.

82
00:04:00,808 --> 00:04:04,918
Wood is cheap, it insulates well, and a
new tank can be built in a single day.

83
00:04:07,284 --> 00:04:10,774
All that water flows into the
city through just two old tunnels:

84
00:04:10,944 --> 00:04:14,474
one finished in nineteen seventeen,
the other in nineteen thirty-six.

85
00:04:15,064 --> 00:04:18,034
And for decades, engineers
have had a terrifying thought.

86
00:04:18,264 --> 00:04:19,334
What if one of them failed?

87
00:04:20,337 --> 00:04:22,837
Nobody could ever shut
them down to inspect them,

88
00:04:22,877 --> 00:04:25,747
because millions of people
depend on them every second.

89
00:04:26,037 --> 00:04:29,207
So in nineteen seventy, the city
started building a third one:

90
00:04:29,397 --> 00:04:30,907
City Water Tunnel Number Three.

91
00:04:31,795 --> 00:04:33,915
It's being dug hundreds
of feet under the city,

92
00:04:33,955 --> 00:04:37,505
through solid rock, by tunnel
workers known as sandhogs.

93
00:04:37,815 --> 00:04:40,475
They blast and drill in the
dark, far below the streets,

94
00:04:40,515 --> 00:04:43,145
and most New Yorkers have
no idea they're there.

95
00:04:43,966 --> 00:04:47,376
It's one of the biggest construction
projects in the city's history.

96
00:04:47,646 --> 00:04:49,796
Twenty-four people have died building it.

97
00:04:50,006 --> 00:04:53,176
The Manhattan section was finally
switched on in twenty thirteen.

98
00:04:54,254 --> 00:04:56,344
And the whole tunnel still isn't finished.

99
00:04:56,654 --> 00:05:00,654
More than fifty years after work began,
the last sections are expected in the

100
00:05:00,694 --> 00:05:01,624
twenty-thirties.

101
00:05:03,749 --> 00:05:05,749
Above the water tunnels,
and below the streets,

102
00:05:05,789 --> 00:05:08,159
runs the most famous thing under New York:

103
00:05:08,269 --> 00:05:08,799
the subway.

104
00:05:09,472 --> 00:05:13,692
It's one of the very few big subway systems
in the world that runs twenty-four hours

105
00:05:13,732 --> 00:05:15,502
a day, every day of the year.

106
00:05:15,692 --> 00:05:18,202
It has four hundred and
seventy-two stations.

107
00:05:19,190 --> 00:05:21,380
And it has a secret enemy: water.

108
00:05:21,870 --> 00:05:24,020
Parts of the subway lie
below the water table.

109
00:05:24,170 --> 00:05:28,510
Even on a dry day, workers have to pump
around thirteen million gallons of water out

110
00:05:28,550 --> 00:05:29,140
of the tunnels.

111
00:05:29,450 --> 00:05:32,280
If the pumps stopped, parts of
the system would start to flood.

112
00:05:33,200 --> 00:05:35,670
In October twenty twelve, the sea came in.

113
00:05:36,260 --> 00:05:39,240
Hurricane Sandy pushed a wall of
salt water into Lower Manhattan,

114
00:05:39,280 --> 00:05:40,990
and it poured into the subway.

115
00:05:41,480 --> 00:05:43,430
Seven tunnels under
the East River flooded.

116
00:05:43,660 --> 00:05:46,750
At South Ferry, the station filled
from the tracks to the ceiling.

117
00:05:47,716 --> 00:05:51,476
The head of the transit authority called
it the worst disaster in the subway's

118
00:05:51,516 --> 00:05:52,046
history.

119
00:05:52,516 --> 00:05:54,706
Salt water ate into
the signals and cables,

120
00:05:54,796 --> 00:05:56,386
and some of the repairs took years.

121
00:05:57,329 --> 00:05:59,959
The subway also hides
some beautiful secrets.

122
00:06:00,449 --> 00:06:03,409
Under City Hall, there's a station
that opened in nineteen-oh-four,

123
00:06:03,449 --> 00:06:07,219
with arched tile ceilings,
skylights and brass chandeliers.

124
00:06:08,136 --> 00:06:12,476
It closed in nineteen forty-five, because
its curved platform was too short for

125
00:06:12,516 --> 00:06:13,846
newer, longer trains.

126
00:06:13,936 --> 00:06:15,086
But it's still there.

127
00:06:15,536 --> 00:06:18,786
If you stay on the number six
train after its last stop downtown,

128
00:06:18,936 --> 00:06:22,316
the train loops through the old station,
and for a few seconds you can see it from

129
00:06:22,356 --> 00:06:22,866
the window.

130
00:06:25,216 --> 00:06:27,036
And under all of it,
the subway, the water,

131
00:06:27,076 --> 00:06:28,366
the steam, there's the rock.

132
00:06:28,879 --> 00:06:31,519
Much of Manhattan sits
on an extremely hard,

133
00:06:31,559 --> 00:06:33,829
ancient rock called Manhattan schist.

134
00:06:34,099 --> 00:06:37,769
In places like Central Park, you can
still see it breaking through the grass.

135
00:06:38,679 --> 00:06:43,019
For decades, people said this is why
Manhattan has two clusters of skyscrapers,

136
00:06:43,059 --> 00:06:46,729
in Midtown and Downtown: that's where
the rock is close to the surface.

137
00:06:47,692 --> 00:06:51,462
But when economists mapped the bedrock,
they found the story is more complicated.

138
00:06:51,852 --> 00:06:54,622
The rock between the two clusters
isn't much deeper at all.

139
00:06:54,812 --> 00:06:57,692
The towers went where the money
was: banks and Wall Street downtown,

140
00:06:57,732 --> 00:07:00,762
and offices around the great
train stations in Midtown.

141
00:07:01,032 --> 00:07:04,082
Where the rock was deeper,
engineers simply dug further down.

142
00:07:04,384 --> 00:07:06,894
Digging down has always
been dangerous here.

143
00:07:07,144 --> 00:07:09,644
To build the Brooklyn Bridge
in the eighteen seventies,

144
00:07:09,684 --> 00:07:14,194
workers dug under the river inside huge
wooden boxes filled with compressed air.

145
00:07:14,444 --> 00:07:17,444
When they came back up too fast,
many got a mysterious illness,

146
00:07:17,484 --> 00:07:19,374
with terrible pain in their joints.

147
00:07:19,867 --> 00:07:21,197
Today we call it the bends.

148
00:07:21,507 --> 00:07:24,077
More than a hundred workers
fell ill, and some died.

149
00:07:24,447 --> 00:07:26,787
Even the chief engineer,
Washington Roebling,

150
00:07:26,827 --> 00:07:29,517
was left partly paralyzed,
and his wife, Emily,

151
00:07:29,567 --> 00:07:31,337
helped carry the project to the finish.

152
00:07:33,777 --> 00:07:35,307
So, what's hidden under New York City?

153
00:07:35,862 --> 00:07:39,912
A hundred-year-old steam system, heating
buildings through pipes under the streets.

154
00:07:40,362 --> 00:07:44,352
Water flowing by gravity from mountains
a hundred and twenty-five miles away.

155
00:07:44,662 --> 00:07:46,842
A tunnel that has taken
half a century to dig.

156
00:07:46,882 --> 00:07:50,892
And a subway that never sleeps,
kept dry by pumps that never stop.

157
00:07:51,728 --> 00:07:55,538
Every day, millions of people walk over
all of it without a single thought.

158
00:07:55,748 --> 00:07:58,798
And that's exactly how a city
like this is supposed to run.

159
00:07:59,221 --> 00:08:01,211
So, would you take a tour under New York?

160
00:08:01,301 --> 00:08:03,151
And which city should we explain next?

161
00:08:03,261 --> 00:08:04,211
Tell me in the comments.

162
00:08:04,561 --> 00:08:05,671
This was How It's Run.
